1. What is Version Control System (VCS)?
A Version Control System is a tool that tracks and manages changes in files over time, mainly used for source code..

Maintains history of changes
Allows collaboration between multiple users
Helps in rollback (restore previous version)
Tracks who made changes and when
Automation  triggers

✅ One-line: VCS is used to track, manage, and control changes in files efficiently.

2. How many types of VCS?
There are 3 types of Version Control Systems:


Local VCS

Works on a single system
No collaboration
Example: RCS



Centralized VCS (CVCS)

One central server stores code
Users commit to central repo
Example: SVN, CVS



Distributed VCS (DVCS)

Each user has full copy of repo
Supports offline work
Example: Git




3. Difference between DVCS & CVCS
DVCS:

No central dependency (each user has full repo)
Works offline
Faster operations
Better branching & merging
Example: Git

CVCS:

Depends on central server
Requires network connection
Slower compared to DVCS
Limited branching support
Example: SVN

✅ Key point (interview):
DVCS is more reliable and flexible than CVCS because it does not depend on a central server.

4. What is Git Merge?
Git Merge is used to combine changes from one branch into another branch.

Integrates feature branch into main branch
Creates a merge commit (in most cases)
Keeps complete branch history

✅ Example:

Feature branch → merge into main

✅ One-line: Git Merge combines changes from one branch to another while preserving history.

5. What is Git Rebase?
Git Rebase is used to move or reapply commits on top of another branch.

Rewrites commit history
Makes linear/clean history
No merge commit created

✅ Example:

Feature branch commits moved on top of latest main


✅ Merge vs Rebase (quick interview point)

Merge → keeps history, creates merge commit
Rebase → rewrites history, cleaner linear flow

6. What is squash in Git?
Git squash is used to combine multiple commits into a single commit.

Helps keep commit history clean and readable
Commonly used before merging feature branch
Reduces unnecessary small commits

✅ Command:
git merge --squash feature
✅ Example:

5 commits in feature branch → squash → 1 commit in main

✅ One-line: Squash merges multiple commits into one to maintain a clean commit history.

7. What is stash in Git?
Git stash is used to temporarily save uncommitted changes and clean the working directory.

Saves work-in-progress (WIP)
Useful when switching branches quickly
Restores changes later

✅ Commands:
git stash
git stash pop
git stash list
✅ Example:

You made changes but not committed → stash → switch branch → come back → apply stash

✅ One-line: Git stash temporarily stores uncommitted changes without committing them.

8. What is git reset?
Git reset is used to undo commits or staging changes by moving the HEAD pointer.
✅ Types:


--soft

Undo commit, keep changes staged
Example: git reset --soft HEAD~1



(default --mixed)

Undo commit, unstage changes
Code stays in working directory



--hard

Deletes commit + staging + working directory changes
Dangerous (data loss)



✅ Use case:

Commit message wrong or commit mistake → fix using reset

✅ One-line: Git reset moves HEAD to undo commits and control staging/working directory changes.

9. What is git revert?
Git revert is used to undo changes of a previous commit without deleting history.

Creates a new commit
Safe for shared branches
Does not remove old commit

✅ Command:
git revert 
✅ Example:

Fix production issue by reverting bad commit

✅ One-line: Git revert creates a new commit that reverses the changes of a previous commit.

✅ Reset vs Revert (very important interview point)


Reset

Rewrites history
Can delete commits
Not safe for shared branches



Revert

Keeps history intact
Creates new commit
Safe for production use

10. Difference between git reset and git revert
Git Reset:

Moves HEAD pointer
Deletes or modifies commit history
Can remove commits permanently
Not safe for shared/production branches
Used for local undo

Git Revert:

Does NOT delete history
Creates a new commit to undo changes
Safe for shared/production branches
Keeps full audit history

✅ Key point (interview):
Reset rewrites history, Revert preserves history.

11. What is git fetch?
Git fetch is used to download latest changes from remote repository without merging them.

Updates local copy of remote branches
Does NOT change your working code
Safe operation

✅ Command:
git fetch origin
✅ One-line: Fetch gets latest changes but does not apply them.

12. What is git pull?
Git pull is used to fetch + merge changes from remote repository into current branch.

Combines fetch + merge
Updates your working directory
Can create merge conflicts

✅ Command:
git pull origin main
✅ One-line: Pull fetches and directly applies changes to your branch.

13. Difference between fetch and pull
Fetch:

Only downloads changes
Does not modify working directory
Safe to use anytime
Manual merge required

Pull:

Downloads + merges changes
Updates working directory
Can cause conflicts
Automatic operation

✅ Key point:
Fetch = safe preview, Pull = direct update

14. What is git conflict?
Git conflict occurs when two changes affect the same file/line and Git cannot decide which one to keep.
✅ When it happens:

During merge
During pull
During rebase

✅ Example:

You modify line 10
Another developer also modifies same line
→ conflict occurs

✅ How to resolve:

Edit file manually
Remove conflict markers
Choose correct code
Commit again

✅ One-line: Git conflict happens when multiple changes overlap and require manual resolution.


15. What is git cherry-pick?
Git cherry-pick is used to apply a specific commit from one branch to another.

Useful for hotfix / bugfix
Does not merge entire branch
Copies only selected commit

✅ Command:
git cherry-pick 
✅ Example:

Apply one bug fix commit from dev → main

✅ One-line: Cherry-pick applies a specific commit from one branch to another.

16. What is .gitignore?
.gitignore is a file used to exclude files and directories from being tracked by Git.

Prevents unnecessary files from being committed
Common for logs, temp files, passwords

✅ Example entries:
node_modules/
*.log
.env
✅ One-line: .gitignore tells Git which files to ignore and not track.

17. What is git reflog?
Git reflog shows history of all HEAD movements, even deleted commits.

Tracks branch switches, resets, rebases
Helps recover lost commits

✅ Command:
git reflog
✅ Example:

Recover accidentally deleted commit using reflog

✅ One-line: Reflog helps track HEAD changes and recover lost commits.

18. What is git blame?
Git blame shows who last modified each line of a file.

Helps identify responsible developer
Useful for debugging

✅ Command:
git blame 
✅ One-line: Git blame displays author information for each line in a file.

19. What is git config?
Git config is used to set Git configuration values (user, email, editor, etc.)

Can be set at system, global, or local level
Stores user identity and preferences

✅ Commands:
git config --global user.name "Rizwan"
git config --global user.email "devops.rmk@gmail.com"
✅ One-line: Git config sets user identity and configuration settings.

20. What is detached HEAD state?
Detached HEAD state occurs when HEAD points to a commit instead of a branch.

Happens when checking out a specific commit
Changes are not saved to any branch unless explicitly created

✅ Example:
git checkout 
✅ One-line: Detached HEAD means you are not on a branch but directly on a commit.

21. What is git clean?
Git clean is used to remove untracked files from the working directory.

Cleans unwanted files
Does not affect tracked files

✅ Commands:
git clean -n   → preview
git clean -f   → force delete
✅ One-line: Git clean removes untracked files from the working directory.
